C# Advanced Terminal (CAT) Terms of Use and Contribution Guide

This document is an extension of the MIT License that governs the use and distribution of the C# Advanced Terminal (CAT). While the MIT License protects the project from a legal standpoint, this guide outlines the ethical principles and community expectations for all users and contributors.

1. Purpose and Philosophy

CAT is an open-source, community-driven project designed to provide a flexible and powerful terminal environment. Its core philosophy is to empower users with a versatile tool for automation, system administration, and development by leveraging the capabilities of C#. The project is built on the principles of accessibility, transparency, and collaboration.

2. Open-Source and Community Ownership

As an open-source project, CAT's ownership is shared among its community. lunaNoir, as the original author, have contributed the initial code, but the project's future is shaped by collective effort. This means:

    No one person or entity holds exclusive control over the project.

    Contributions from all individuals are valued and encouraged.

    Decisions regarding the project's direction will be made through open discussion and community consensus.

3. Acceptable Use Policy

Due to its nature as a command-line shell, CAT has the ability to execute code and interact with the operating system. This power comes with a responsibility. While the project and its code are provided "as is" under the MIT License, users are expected to adhere to the following principles:

    Do not use CAT for malicious or illegal purposes. This includes, but is not limited to, unauthorized access to systems, data theft, or any activity that violates local, national, or international laws.

    Be mindful of the security implications. The dynamic code compilation feature could be used to execute arbitrary C# code. Users are responsible for vetting any scripts or "bundles" they run with CAT to prevent security vulnerabilities.

    Respect others and their systems. Use CAT in a way that does not cause harm, disrupt services, or violate the privacy of others.

4. Contribution Guidelines

We welcome contributions from everyone, regardless of their skill level or background. By contributing to CAT, you agree to the following:

    Your contributions will be licensed under the MIT License. This ensures that the project remains open and free for everyone to use and modify.

    Follow the project's coding standards. This helps maintain code quality and readability.

    Be respectful and constructive in all interactions. We expect all community members to be professional and courteous, whether in bug reports, feature requests, or discussions.

    Clearly document your contributions. Explain what your code does and why it's necessary. This makes it easier for others to understand and review your work.

